      "On the island of Bohol in the central Philippines, more than a thousand rounded hills rise from the flat farmland like the bumps on a giant egg carton. Most of them are between about 30 and 50 meters high, and a few are much taller. They are covered with grass, and during the dry season, when the rains stop, the grass dries out and turns brown. At that time of year, the hills look like rows of chocolate drops, which is how they got their famous name, the Chocolate Hills.",
      "Local people have told several stories to explain where the hills came from. In one, two giants fought for days by throwing rocks and sand at each other, and when they finally made peace, they left the battlefield without cleaning up. In another, a giant named Arogo fell in love with a human woman, and after she died, the tears he cried dried into the hills. Such stories are still shared with visitors to the island today.",
      "Scientists explain the hills differently, although they have not agreed on every detail. The hills are made mostly of limestone, a rock that forms from the shells and skeletons of sea creatures, and this suggests that the area was once under a shallow sea. Over a very long time, movements of the earth lifted the limestone above the water. According to one common explanation, rain then slowly dissolved and wore away the rock, leaving rounded hills in the places where it resisted.",
      "The hills are spread over an area of roughly 50 square kilometers, and from a viewing deck built on top of one of them, visitors can see them stretching toward the horizon. Seen from there, the scene can look like an empty natural landscape made of nothing but hills. ( A ), the land between them is busy with human activity. Farmers grow rice and other crops in the flat fields, and many families live in villages scattered among the hills.",
      "In 1988, the Philippine government declared the Chocolate Hills a national geological monument, and the area has since become one of the country's best-known tourist sites. However, protecting the hills has not always been easy. In 2013, a powerful earthquake struck Bohol, and parts of some hills collapsed in landslides. In 2024, many people were upset when photographs spread online showing a resort with a swimming pool built at the foot of several hills inside the protected area.",
      "The case led to calls for stronger rules and closer checks on building in the area. Supporters of tourism point out that visitors bring jobs and income to local families. Others argue that the hills, which took a very long time to form, could be damaged within a few years by careless development. Finding a balance between the two may be the real challenge for the people of Bohol, whose famous hills are valued both as a landscape and as a way of making a living.",
