    id: 'adv-margarine-colour-laws',
    title: 'The Long Battle over the Colour of Margarine',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the 1860s, butter was becoming expensive in France, partly because the country's growing cities needed more food than its farms could easily supply. The emperor, Napoleon III, is said to have offered a prize to anyone who could produce a cheap substitute that would keep well and could feed sailors and poorer families. A French chemist named Hippolyte Mège-Mouriès took up the challenge. In 1869, he patented a spread made mainly from beef fat mixed with milk, which became known as oleomargarine.",
      "The new product soon spread beyond France. Companies in the Netherlands began producing it in large quantities, and before long it was being made in Britain, Germany and the United States as well. Because it cost much less than butter, it was welcomed by working families, who could now afford a rich spread for their bread. Dairy farmers, however, saw it as a serious threat to their incomes, and they soon began to fight back.",
      "In the United States, the fight was carried on mainly through laws. In 1886, the federal government placed a special tax on margarine, and many states passed rules of their own. Since margarine was naturally a pale, whitish colour, the most important battles were about whether makers could dye it yellow so that it looked like butter. Dairy supporters argued that yellow margarine deceived customers, while margarine makers replied that butter itself was often coloured to make it look richer. The argument was not only about honesty, of course, since both sides knew that shoppers were far more likely to buy a spread that looked familiar.",
      "Some of the rules went surprisingly far. Around the end of the nineteenth century, a few states passed laws requiring margarine to be coloured pink, so that no one could possibly mistake it for butter. ( A ), the Supreme Court ruled in 1898 that such a law went too far, since it made the product so unattractive that hardly anyone would buy it. Many states nevertheless continued to ban yellow margarine, and from 1902 a higher federal tax on coloured margarine made yellow products far more expensive than white ones.",
      "Makers and shoppers soon found ways around these rules. Margarine was often sold white, together with a small capsule of yellow dye that the buyer could mix in at home. During the Second World War, when butter was scarce, many American households spent time squeezing and kneading plastic packets until the margarine inside turned an even yellow. The job was tiresome, and it is often remembered by people who did it as children in those years.",
      "After the war, the old rules slowly collapsed as margarine became an everyday food. The federal tax was ended by a law passed in 1950, and one state after another allowed yellow margarine to be sold. Wisconsin, a major dairy state, is usually said to have been the last to drop its ban, in 1967. The long struggle suggests that the colour of a food can matter almost as much as its taste, because people often judge what they eat with their eyes before they put it in their mouths. Today few shoppers remember that the appearance of this ordinary product was once a matter for courts and lawmakers.",
    ],
