    id: 'adv-water-rocket-thrust',
    title: 'A Plastic Bottle That Obeys Newton',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A water rocket is one of the simplest flying machines that a student can build. It is usually made from an empty plastic drink bottle, partly filled with water and turned upside down on a launcher that seals its mouth. Air is pumped into the bottle with an ordinary bicycle pump until the pressure inside is several times higher than that of the atmosphere. When the seal is released, water rushes out of the narrow opening and the bottle shoots upward, sometimes climbing tens of meters into the sky.",
      "The principle behind this flight was described by Isaac Newton more than three hundred years ago. His third law of motion states that when one object pushes on another, the second object pushes back on the first. This return push is equal in size and acts in the opposite direction. The compressed air drives the water out of the bottle downward, and the water in turn pushes the bottle upward with an equal force. Real rockets work in the same way, although they throw out hot gases produced by burning fuel instead of water.",
      "A common question is why the water is needed at all. A bottle that contains only compressed air will also fly when it is released, but it usually rises only a short distance before falling back. The reason is that air is very light, so although it leaves the bottle quickly, it carries little momentum away with it. Water is roughly eight hundred times denser than air at ordinary pressure. Pushing it out at high speed therefore produces a much larger thrust on the bottle during the short time that the push lasts.",
      "This does not mean that more water is always better. If the bottle is completely full of water, there is no room for compressed air, which acts like a spring that drives the water out. ( A ), if there is too little water, the push ends almost as soon as it begins and the rocket gains little speed. Between these extremes lies a best amount. Experiments and calculations commonly suggest that filling roughly a third of the bottle gives the greatest height, although the exact figure depends on the pressure, the bottle and the nozzle.",
      "The flight itself has two very different stages. During the first, which lasts only a fraction of a second, the rocket is driven by the escaping water and speeds up rapidly. As the water leaves, the rocket also becomes lighter, so a push of the same strength produces an even greater increase in speed. Once the water is gone, a brief burst of air follows. After that, the rocket simply coasts upward, slowed by gravity and by air resistance, until it stops for a moment at the top of its path and begins to fall.",
      "A rocket that tumbles through the air wastes much of its energy, so builders pay close attention to stability. Most water rockets have fins attached near the mouth of the bottle, which points backward during flight. Many builders also add a little weight to the nose. The aim is to keep the center of mass, where the rocket's weight is effectively concentrated, ahead of the center of pressure, where the forces of the air effectively act. Then any small turn away from the flight path creates a force that swings the nose back into line, much as feathers keep an arrow pointing forward.",
      "What makes the water rocket especially instructive is that it contains, in miniature, a trade-off faced by the engineers of real launch vehicles. Every extra amount of propellant adds to the push, but it also adds to the weight that must be lifted. Beyond a certain point, more fuel brings smaller and smaller benefits. A student searching for the best amount of water in a plastic bottle is therefore, in a small way, working on the same problem that has shaped the design of rockets sent into space.",
    ],
