    id: 'todai-printed-restaurant-menus',
    title: 'Should Restaurants Have to Offer Printed Menus?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Over the past few years, many restaurants have replaced printed menus with a small square code on each table. Customers scan the code with the camera of a phone, and the menu opens on the screen. The practice spread quickly during the COVID-19 pandemic, when restaurants wanted to reduce the number of objects that many people touched. In some places it has remained common even after those concerns faded, and some restaurants now offer no printed menu at all. This has led to a proposal that restaurants should be required to provide a printed menu to any customer who asks for one. Is such a requirement justified?",
      "Supporters argue that eating out should not depend on owning a working smartphone. Some customers, many of them older, do not have one, and others may find that their battery is empty or that the signal inside a building is weak. People with poor eyesight may struggle to read long lists of dishes on a small screen, especially in dim light. For such customers, a restaurant without printed menus can be awkward or even embarrassing to visit. Supporters say that a meal out is a basic social activity from which nobody should be quietly shut out.",
      "Supporters also raise concerns about privacy. Some online menus are linked to ordering systems that ask customers to create an account or that collect data about what they view and buy. A customer who simply wants to know the price of a dish may not expect to share information with a company they have never heard of. A printed menu, by contrast, records nothing. Supporters add that when each person at a table looks at a separate small screen, it becomes ( A ) for the group to discuss together what to order.",
      "Opponents reply that digital menus have real advantages for restaurants. Printed menus must be redesigned and reprinted whenever prices or dishes change, which can happen often when the cost of ingredients rises. A digital menu can be updated in minutes, and it can show photographs, list allergens in detail and appear in several languages. Some systems also let staff mark a dish as sold out at once, so that customers are not disappointed after ordering. For a small restaurant with little money to spare, these savings may matter. Opponents argue that a legal requirement would add a cost that falls hardest on the businesses least able to bear it.",
      "A second objection is about how such a rule would work in practice. Opponents ask what would count as a menu, whether a single laminated sheet, a board on the wall or a member of staff reading out the dishes. If the rule is vague, it may be ignored, and if it is detailed, it may become a burden for inspectors as well as owners. Opponents also point out that the number of customers without phones is likely to shrink over time. In their view, the market will solve the problem, since restaurants that turn customers away will lose business.",
      "The argument about cost is weaker than it sounds. A printed menu need not be a glossy booklet, and a few sheets printed in the restaurant's office would satisfy most customers who ask. The point about practical detail is more serious, because a badly written rule could be both annoying and ineffective. The claim that the market will solve the problem, however, overlooks how small the affected group may be. A restaurant can lose a few customers without noticing, while those customers may find that the same barrier appears almost everywhere they go.",
      "On balance, a requirement is justified, but it should be simple. Restaurants could be required to keep at least one readable printed copy of their current menu and prices, to be handed to any customer on request. A board on the wall showing the same information should also count. Nothing in such a rule would prevent restaurants from offering digital menus as well. The aim is not to resist new technology but to ensure that a meal out remains open to everyone who walks through the door.",
    ],
