      text: 'A home printer is often sold cheaply, and the money is really made on the ink it needs afterwards. To keep that income, some makers build in a chip and use updates so that the machine will ( 1 ) ink that is not their own. They defend this by pointing to the low price of the printer and to the ( 2 ) of their matched ink, while opponents call it a way of holding a captive market. The writer would allow a maker to advise against other ink but not to ( 3 ) a part that has already been working.',
