    id: 'todai-power-lines-underground',
    title: 'Should Japan Move Its Power Lines Underground?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Walk down almost any street in a Japanese city and you will see concrete poles carrying a tangle of electric and telephone wires overhead. Japan is often said to have tens of millions of such poles, and their number has been reported to keep rising rather than falling. In cities such as London and Paris, by contrast, nearly all the lines in central areas run underground. A law passed in 2016 calls for poles to be removed from roads step by step, but progress has been slow. The question is whether Japan should spend much more to move its overhead lines below the ground.",
      "Supporters begin with safety in disasters. Strong earthquakes and typhoons can bring poles down across roads, cutting power and blocking the way for ambulances, fire engines and people escaping on foot. When a powerful typhoon struck the Kanto region in 2019, many poles in Chiba Prefecture were broken or blown over, and some households went without electricity for more than a week. Cables in the ground, supporters argue, are protected from wind and falling trees, and they leave the road clear when it is needed most.",
      "Supporters also point to everyday life. On many narrow streets in Japan, poles stand on the edge of the road or in the middle of the pavement, forcing people with pushchairs and wheelchairs to step into traffic. Removing them would widen the space that pedestrians can actually use. There is also the question of appearance. Towns that hope to attract visitors to historic streets have found that buried lines make old buildings and distant views far more striking. Photographs of such streets no longer show a web of wires across every scene.",
      "Opponents reply that the main obstacle is cost. Laying cables underground requires digging trenches, building concrete channels and installing equipment that would otherwise hang on poles. As a result, it is generally said to cost many times as much as overhead lines over the same distance. Much of this cost falls on local governments and power companies, and in the end on taxpayers and customers. In towns where the population is shrinking, opponents ask whether such spending can be justified when roads, water pipes and bridges also need repair.",
      "A second objection concerns maintenance. When an overhead line fails, workers can usually see the fault and repair it from a truck within hours. A fault in a buried cable is harder to locate, and the road may have to be dug up to reach it. Underground equipment can also be damaged by flooding, and the transformers that once sat at the top of poles must be placed in large boxes on the pavement, which can themselves ( A ) narrow walkways. Opponents conclude that buried lines are not always more reliable, only vulnerable in different ways.",
      "Between these positions, engineers have been looking for cheaper methods. One approach is to bury cables at shallower depths than older rules required, which reduces the amount of digging. Another is to lay cables directly in the soil without concrete channels, as is done in some other countries. Some towns have also chosen to run lines along back streets instead of main roads. Others focus their efforts on emergency routes and on the areas around hospitals and schools, where the benefits are greatest. Engineers are also developing smaller transformer boxes, which take up less room on narrow pavements and leave more space for the people walking past them.",
      "In my view, Japan should continue to move lines underground, but it should be selective about where it begins. Routes that ambulances and evacuees will depend on after an earthquake deserve priority, as do narrow streets used by many children and older people. Elsewhere, the cheaper methods now being tested should be adopted widely before costly ones are chosen by default. Poles will not vanish from every street in my lifetime, yet each one removed from an emergency route makes a city a little safer when disaster strikes. A slower plan that puts the most important streets first will achieve more than an ambitious one that cannot be paid for.",
    ],
