    qa: [
      { q: 'What did flexible celluloid film allow cameras to do?', choices: ['Take pictures without any light at all', 'Hold film that could be rolled up', 'Record sound together with every picture', 'Use glass plates that were much thinner'], answer: 1, why: '第4段落 Such film could be rolled up, so cameras no longer needed to be loaded with heavy glass plates one at a time。' },
      { q: 'Why do many archives keep old reels in cool, separate vaults?', choices: ['Old film becomes too heavy for normal shelves', 'Cool air makes the pictures look much brighter', 'Visitors are not allowed to watch nitrate film', 'Decaying film may catch fire more easily'], answer: 3, why: '第5段落 As it decays, it releases gases and becomes even more likely to catch fire, which is why many archives keep old reels in cool, separate vaults。' },
    ],
