      text: 'Since April 2024, heirs in Japan have had a legal duty to register land and buildings that they inherit, and those who fail to do so without good reason can be fined. Supporters say that clear ownership helps disaster recovery and public works and that delay only makes matters harder as heirs ( 1 ), while critics point out that the costs can exceed the value of remote land and that the rules may not be applied ( 2 ). The writer supports the duty but wants registration to be cheaper and fines to be used only as a last ( 3 ).',
