    id: 'todai-japan-emergency-bear-shooting-in-towns',
    title: 'Should Mayors Be Able to Order Bears Shot in Towns?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In recent years, bears have appeared more and more often in Japanese towns, especially in the north of the country. In fiscal 2023, more people were injured or killed by bears than in any earlier year on record. Until recently, however, the law made it hard to deal with a bear once it had entered a town. Firing a gun in residential areas was generally banned, and a hunter could usually shoot only if a police officer ordered it in an emergency. In 2025, the Diet revised the wildlife protection law to create a system of emergency shooting, which took effect that September. The change has been welcomed, but it has raised difficult questions.",
      "Under the new system, a mayor can let hunters shoot a dangerous animal, such as a bear or a wild boar, where people live. Four conditions must be met: the animal must have entered an area of daily life or be likely to do so, and urgent action must be needed. It must also be difficult to capture the animal quickly and reliably by any other means, and there must be no risk that a bullet will hit a person. To meet the last condition, the municipality can stop traffic and tell residents to stay indoors or leave the area. If a bullet damages a house or a car, the municipality, not the hunter, pays compensation.",
      "Supporters argue first that the change saves time when time matters most. Under the old arrangement, officials, police and hunters were sometimes unclear about who should take responsibility while a bear moved through a neighborhood. Some bears hid in buildings for hours while residents stayed indoors. With clear conditions written into law, a mayor can now make a decision on the spot. Supporters also point out that a clear rule ( A ) hunters, who used to worry that they might be blamed or lose their gun licenses after shooting in a town.",
      "Their second argument is that the danger itself has changed. Bears that have learned to find food near houses may return again and again, and they appear less afraid of people than before. Traps and loud noises can drive such an animal away for a while, but they rarely solve the problem for good. Supporters stress that the system is not meant for the routine culling of wildlife but is a last step for when people face an animal that could kill them. In their view, refusing to allow shooting in such cases would put residents at greater risk without saving many bears.",
      "Critics, however, question whether there are enough people to carry out the law. Emergency shooting depends on local hunters, most of whom are private citizens with other jobs. Many are older, and a large share of those who hold hunting licenses are over sixty. Shooting a large animal in a street demands far more skill and nerve than hunting in a forest. The payments that municipalities offer for such work are often small compared with the danger. Some hunting groups have said that their members may turn down requests if they are not given better support.",
      "A second concern is legal responsibility. In one well-known case in Sunagawa, Hokkaido, a hunter shot a bear at a city's request in 2018. The Hokkaido public safety commission later revoked the hunter's gun license, saying that the bullet could have struck a building. The dispute went to court, and judges at different levels reached different conclusions. Critics say that such cases have made hunters cautious, and that a new law cannot remove that fear at once. They also warn that mayors, who are not experts on bears, will have to judge the four conditions in minutes.",
      "In my view, the new system is necessary, but it cannot work on goodwill alone, and municipalities should not rely on a few aging volunteers. They should instead train and employ staff who can respond to bears. The government should also make clear that hunters who follow a mayor's lawful order will not lose their licenses for doing so. Above all, shooting should remain the last step, after efforts to remove the food that draws bears into towns. A rule that allows a gun to be fired is only as good as the people asked to fire it.",
    ],
